CP(1)                         Funny CLI Manual                         CP(1)

NAME
    cp, copy — Copy files and directories, because sometimes Ctrl+C
               and Ctrl+V need a command-line lifestyle.

SYNOPSIS
    cp <src> <dst>
    copy <src> <dst>

DESCRIPTION
    The 'cp' command copies a file or an entire directory from one
    location to another.

    For files, it creates the destination's parent directories if
    necessary and copies the file along with its metadata.

    For directories, it copies the entire directory tree.

    In other words, 'cp' takes something you already have and creates
    another one somewhere else. Capitalism, but for files.

ALIASES
    copy
        An alias for 'cp'.

        For users who prefer commands that explain themselves.

ARGUMENTS
    src
        The source file or directory.

        This is the thing you want more of.

    dst
        The destination path.

        This is where the new thing will live.

        Ideally, somewhere you actually intended.

BEHAVIOR
    If 'src' is a directory:

        The entire directory tree is copied to 'dst'.

    If 'src' is a file:

        The destination's parent directory is created if necessary,
        then the file is copied along with its metadata.

    The command prints:

        Copied

    upon success.

    That's it. No list of files. No ceremony. Just "Copied."

ERRORS
    cp error: <error>
        Something went wrong during the copy.

        Possible causes include:
        - The source doesn't exist.
        - The destination already exists in a way that prevents copying.
        - Permission denied.
        - The filesystem ran out of space.
        - You attempted to copy something that the filesystem considers
          deeply inconvenient.

USAGE ERRORS
    > cp README.md

        Usage: cp <src> <dst>

        A source without a destination is just a cry for help.

    > copy README.md backup.md
        Copied

        Same operation. More words.

EXAMPLES
    > cp notes.txt notes-backup.txt
        Copied

        Congratulations. Your notes now have a backup.

    > cp report.pdf backups/report.pdf
        Copied

        The 'backups' directory will be created if necessary.

        Look at you, planning ahead.

    > cp project project-backup
        Copied

        The entire project has been cloned into another directory.

        May your disk space rest in peace.

    > copy important.txt somewhere/safe.txt
        Copied

        Same as 'cp', but with extra linguistic confidence.

FUN FACTS
    - Files are copied using metadata-preserving copy semantics.
    - Missing parent directories for file destinations are created
      automatically.
    - Directories are copied recursively.
    - The command does not ask for confirmation before copying.
    - There is no progress bar, because sometimes you just have to
      trust the process.
    - 'copy' exists for people who believe command names should be
      immediately understandable.

